WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.292
Many RTIC systems run in `no_std`
environments with strict memory and timing

2
00:00:04.372 --> 00:00:05.072
constraints.

3
00:00:05.152 --> 00:00:09.060
That can mean limited library support,
controlled allocation, fixed buffers,

4
00:00:09.140 --> 00:00:13.049
static resources, and failure behavior
that must match the device rather than a

5
00:00:13.129 --> 00:00:14.471
desktop or server runtime.

6
00:00:14.551 --> 00:00:16.709
Safe Rust reduces memory-corruption risk,

7
00:00:16.789 --> 00:00:20.464
but firmware can still fail through stack
exhaustion, oversized buffers,

8
00:00:20.544 --> 00:00:24.453
unbounded queues, unchecked integer
conversions, uncontrolled allocation,

9
00:00:24.533 --> 00:00:27.391
or panic behavior that is not appropriate
for the product.

10
00:00:27.471 --> 00:00:29.746
Resource safety remains an engineering
task.

11
00:00:29.826 --> 00:00:33.326
Defensive design reviews stack use, heap
policy if any, static memory,

12
00:00:33.406 --> 00:00:37.315
queue lengths, message rates, buffer
sizes, allocation strategy, recursion,

13
00:00:37.395 --> 00:00:38.912
and worst-case parsing paths.

14
00:00:38.992 --> 00:00:42.375
Numeric values that influence indexes,
lengths, timeouts, counts,

15
00:00:42.455 --> 00:00:44.789
or allocation should be validated and
bounded.

16
00:00:44.869 --> 00:00:46.735
Panic behavior should be deliberate.

17
00:00:46.815 --> 00:00:49.616
A development panic policy may be useful
during testing,

18
00:00:49.696 --> 00:00:53.721
but production firmware needs a
product-specific answer for what happens when an

19
00:00:53.801 --> 00:00:54.676
invariant fails.

20
00:00:54.756 --> 00:00:58.664
The answer may involve reset, safe state,
error reporting, watchdog behavior,

21
00:00:58.744 --> 00:01:01.136
or controlled shutdown depending on the
device.

22
00:01:01.216 --> 00:01:04.366
The system should define behavior under
pressure: full queues,

23
00:01:04.446 --> 00:01:08.063
oversized input, dropped messages, missed
timing, peripheral failure,

24
00:01:08.143 --> 00:01:09.368
and allocation failure.

25
00:01:09.448 --> 00:01:13.532
Predictable degraded behavior is better
than pretending those conditions cannot

26
00:01:13.612 --> 00:01:14.020
happen.
